Let me analyze this PHP code carefully.

```php
class P
{
    private int $v = 3;
    public function g(): int { return $this->v; }
}
$p = new P;
$f = $p->g(...);
$p = null;
echo $f();
```

The key here is `$p->g(...)` - this is PHP 8.1's first-class callable syntax. It creates a closure that wraps the method call `$p->g()`.

When `$p->g(...)` is called, it creates a Closure that captures `$p` (the object) internally. The closure holds a reference to the object `$p`.

Then `$p = null` sets the variable `$p` to null, but the closure `$f` still holds a reference to the original `P` object.

So when `$f()` is called, it still has access to the `P` object with `$v = 3`.

Therefore, `echo $f()` will output `3`.

This is the key behavior of first-class callable syntax - the closure captures the object, so even after the original variable is set to null, the object still exists because the closure holds a reference to it.

The answer is 3.

